Replier (
	
	// Source format
	
	- This file defines the Replier First subset for pre-programming.
	- Use only the forms established by this tour before implementation begins.
	- Emit anchors for descriptive content instead of comments.
	- Use double-slash comments only as short headings that group anchors.
	- Use comment headings to break up long runs of anchors.
	- Do not place semantic content in comment headings.
	- An anchor starts with a dash and is semantic content within the declaration it describes.
	- The compiler reads an anchor in its enclosing declaration's context.
	- An anchor governs the declaration that directly encloses it.
	- An anchor records an accepted project decision that constrains later code generation.
	- An imperative anchor is an executable instruction that can be replaced by code at its exact source position.
	- Write imperative anchors as commands that begin with verbs such as Load instead of third-person descriptions such as Loads.
	- Place imperative anchors only inside a function, constructor, or startup body where their replacement code can execute.
	- Imperative anchors and code in the same body execute in source order.
	- A declarative anchor states a fact, invariant, interpretation, or generation constraint instead of an executable instruction.
	- Declarative anchors guide the implementation and are not placeholders for code at their source position.
	- Use declarative wording when an anchor governs its enclosing declaration generally rather than directing an action at that position.
	- A pending question anchor marks a material choice that code generation cannot safely infer without review.
	- Accepting a pending question replaces it with a factual anchor that holds generation to the chosen decision.
	- Do not use question anchors for syntax repairs, generic suggestions, or merely possible implementation alternatives.
	- Place a member's behavior and invariants inside that member instead of its parent.
	- Place an anchor governing a function inside that function's parenthesized body.
	- Do not repeat the enclosing declaration's name in its anchors.
	- Brace-delimited names in an anchor are semantic references to declarations.
	- Reference declarations such as {WebViewHandle} instead of spelling their names in anchor prose.
	- Each anchor contains one concise factual statement.
	- Do not wrap anchor statements across lines.
	- Do not use parentheses for ordinary prose in anchors.
	
	// Formatting
	- Blank lines retain the indentation of their surrounding scope.
	- Do not trim whitespace-only blank lines.
	- Preserved blank-line indentation keeps vertical caret movement in its local column.
	- Commas and newlines are interchangeable separators.
	- Multiple commas, multiple newlines, and mixed comma-newline runs collapse into one separation unit.
	- Prefer newline separation without commas when a parameter, argument, case payload, or other comma-separated list is split across lines.
	
	// Decision rules
	- Public visibility is the default.
	- Use private for private visibility.
	- Do not use static.
	- Ask Paul when a requirement, construct, semantic rule, API shape, or ownership decision is uncertain.
	- Do not invent language features or widen this subset without direction.
	- Do not substitute a familiar TypeScript rule unless this tour establishes it.
	- Organize First by responsibility instead of source file.
	- A space is a named responsibility boundary.
	- Nest spaces instead of encoding hierarchy in names.
	- A declaration without a constructor or inheritance clause is a space.
	- A same-named space beside a class is that class's static side.
	- Place a class's static-side space after its instance-side class.
	
	// Standard library
	- The standard library includes JavaScript and Node.js built-ins when implementation work is enabled.
	- The TypeScript frontend includes browser DOM APIs when implementation work is enabled.
	- Use the ordinary conceptual APIs of standard library built-ins unless a First-specific rule changes them.
	- A template element parses assigned HTML into an inert DocumentFragment.
	- Is introduces types.
	- A function's parenthesized body encloses its anchors and implementation.
	- Add a parenthesized body when a function needs anchors or an implementation.
	- Imports introduce dependencies and are allowed in First source.
	- Use canonical module identifiers when possible.
	- Quote package names containing characters that cannot occur in identifiers and supply an alias.
	- The quoted import above demonstrates a scoped TypeScript package name.
	- Imports precede other semantic members including target declarations.
	- Imports may appear at the root or in spaces and classes but not inside executable bodies.
	- Declare target selects the output backend for the enclosing space and descendants.
	- Declare several targets with separate declarations.
	- Nested explicit targets replace the inherited target set.
	- Backend names are open lowercase identifiers and configuration options belong to the backend.
	- Output paths resolve against the First project root.
	- Raw anchor prose permits letters, marks, numbers, spaces, periods, commas, apostrophes, and hyphens.
	- A question mark is permitted only at the end of a pending question.
	- Place references and resources in supported brace-delimited semantic islands.
	
	// Primitive types
	- Primitive types are explicit.
	- Do not use number for durable data or foreign boundaries when an exact representation is intended.
	- Scalar types are boolean, string, byte string, and char.
	- Integer types are i8, i16, i32, i64, i128, u8, u16, u32, u64, and u128.
	- Choose a concrete integer width for durable data and foreign boundaries.
	- Platform aliases int, uint, decimal, and udecimal select target-platform widths.
	- Number is always f64.
	- Numeric is a compile-time provisional numeric annotation instead of a runtime type.
	- Float types are f16, f32, f64, and f128.
	- A decimal literal is a provisional fixed decimal.
	- Append f for f32 literals.
	- Append ff for f64 literals.
	- Fixed decimals use an integer width followed by a scale.
	- Fixed decimal scales are i8 or u8 from 1 through 3, i16 or u16 from 1 through 5, i32 or u32 from 1 through 10, i64 or u64 from 1 through 19, and i128 or u128 from 1 through 39.
	- Big is an arbitrary-precision signed integer.
	- Big fixed decimal scales range from 1 through 64.
	- Fixed-width integers and fixed decimals wrap on overflow.
	
	import "@supabase/supabase-js" as Supabase
	
	declare target typescript {
		path = "src-typescript"
		options = {
			// Backend-specific compiler options go here
		}
	}
	
	declare target swift {
		path = "src-swift"
	}
	
	formatDocumentName(username is string) is string (
		- Functions have lowercase names and typed parameters.
		- A function return type is optional when reachable returns make it unambiguous.
		- Functions are the reusable executable behavior in this subset.
		return username + ".replier"
	)
	
	placeView(
		x is f64 = ?
		y is f64 = ?
		width is f64 = ?
		height is f64 = ?
		allowsFileAccess is boolean = false
		escapable is boolean = false) (
		- Multiline parameter lists use newlines as separators and do not use comma tokens.
	)
	
	startup (
		- Startup is automatic program startup.
		- Startup has no name, parameters, return type, or call syntax.
		- Place startup beside its owning space.
		- Program-owned startup functions run in program order.
		- Program-owned startup functions do not run when the entry point is a test.
		Launcher.present()
	)
	
	Launcher (
		- Spaces hold responsibility-scoped functions, types, values, starters, and nested spaces.
		- Access spaces by qualification.
		- Spaces cannot be constructed.
		present() (
		)
	)
	
	WebView (
		- Private members are accessible only within the class.
		- Async functions use is async and specify a non-null result type after async.
		- First does not use Promise types.
		- Omit a function body when declaring an API shape only.
		constructor
		reload()
		loadLabel() is async string
	)
	
	WebView (
		- This same-named space is WebView's static side.
		- Static-side spaces do not have constructors.
		createHidden() is WebView
	)
	
	Document (
		- A declaration with constructor is a nominal reference class.
		- Calling a class creates a value.
		- First has no new keyword.
		- A class has at most one constructor.
		- Constructors can explicitly return a compatible instance.
		- Constructors do not have visibility modifiers.
		- Constructors can omit their bodies when declaring class shape only.
		- Stored fields declare state owned by each instance.
		- Every stored field is definitely assigned by the end of construction.
		- A constructor parameter with is field of Type declares and assigns the same-named stored field.
		- Field parameters replace explicit constructor copies for their stored fields.
		status is DocumentStatus
		
		constructor(
			identifier is field of string
			accountName is field of string) (
			this.status = DocumentStatus.open()
		)
		
		close() (
			- Instance functions use this for the current value.
			this.status = DocumentStatus.closed()
		)
	)

	Persistable (
		- Database writes require an explicit persistence operation.
		constructor
	)

	Post is Persistable (
		- A post stores one captured X post.
		- Inherits the database-write rule from {Persistable}.

		constructor(identifier is field of string)
	)

	CaptureWatch (
		- A capture watch repeatedly reads one source.
		constructor(source is field of string)

		start() (
			- Calling start begins repeated reads from {source}.
		)

		stop() (
			- Calling stop prevents further reads after it returns.
		)
	)
	
	WebViewHandle (
		- Declare stored state explicitly and initialize matching fields with constructor field parameters.
		constructor(identifier is field of string)
	)
	
	DocumentStatus is one case of (
		- One case of models a closed lifecycle or result shape.
		- Each case is a constructor.
		- A value has exactly one active case.
		- Cases can carry typed payloads.
		- Match cases when computation syntax is enabled.
		opening()
		open()
		authenticationRequired()
		accountMismatch(expected is string, authenticated is string)
		closed()
	)
	
	WebViewPresentation is one of (
		- One of models one named identity.
		- Entries can have explicit primitive values.
		- Entries without values are inferred.
		- Use one of for a closed single choice.
		hidden
		visible
	)
	
	WebViewOpacity is one of (
		- One of constrains one primitive value from a closed set.
		- Raw values in the set are compatible.
		- The declaration provides named access to set values.
		0
		1
	)
	
	WebViewCapability is many of (
		- Many of models zero or more named flags.
		- Entries without values are inferred as powers of two.
		- Include none when the empty state needs a named value.
		none
		load
		evaluateJavaScript
		resize
		move
		dispose
	)
	
	WebViewRelation (
		- Ownership belongs in stored field types instead of ordinary parameters or local variables.
		- Weak does not retain its target and can become null.
		- Strong explicitly acknowledges a potentially retaining cycle.
		constructor(
			owner is field of weak Document
			peer is field of strong WebViewHandle)
	)
	
	OptionalDocument (
		- An optional ordinary reference retains its target while present.
		- A weak reference permits its target to disappear even while this field exists.
		constructor(
			selected is field of Document or null
			observed is field of weak Document)
	)
	
	- Consult the official documentation before attempting to use features not introduced by this tour.
)
